\section*{A charged enclosing-area bound for weakly asymptotically Euclidean exteriors}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: A combined manuscript claims neutral three- and four-dimensional bounds, three-dimensional charged upper-area bounds, original-data rigidity, and a local anti-de Sitter perturbation inequality; For one-ended three-dimensional source-free electric--magnetic data, the proposed sharp bound is $m\geq Q$ and $r_A\leq m+\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}$, with Reissner--Nordstrom rigidity in the stated horizon subclass. Together they give a claimed affirmative answer in the matching scope.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Prepared-end graph deformations protect the minimum area and compare invariant masses. Conformal flow supplies the Riemannian step, electromagnetic flux converts charge into the scalar comparison, and equality conditions reconstruct the original static spacetime data; the anti-de Sitter result uses a separate local conformal expansion.

A coupled filled scalar deformation retains enclosing area while accounting for electromagnetic flux in the mass balance. The neutral comparison gives the upper-area branch, and saturation of the comparison identities reconstructs a dyonic spacetime slice carrying the given metric and extrinsic curvature.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The synthesis charged theorem gives the three-dimensional mass/charge/enclosing-area inequalities for its future-trapped charged exterior. Its rigidity theorem is neutral; the target charged RN original-data rigidity and mixed trapped-sign extension are not supplied. For one-ended three-dimensional source-free electric--magnetic data, the proposed sharp bound is $m\geq Q$ and $r_A\leq m+\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}$, with Reissner--Nordstrom rigidity in the stated horizon subclass. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 260, the only selected family entry is the CKS end-replacement construction and Schwarzschild equality examples. A connected-boundary inequality is conditional on the flat Penrose input; the general Bondi or spacetime inequalities are not formalized there. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
